# AMD64 thunk export spec -- d3dx9_42.dll.
#
# From the 2026-08-17 Skyrim Special Edition triage, and the same finding as
# the 2026-08-15 one that added dxgi's and d3d11's: SkyrimSE.exe imports this
# module, Wine provides it as a NATIVE ppc64 builtin, and the loader correctly
# refuses to splice that into a guest call -- reported as c000007b, four times
# over, before the process reaches a single graphics call.  Nothing here is
# hand-written: the export list comes from this module's own .spec.
DLL d3dx9_42.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# THE MODULE LOADED AND EXPORTED ONE THING.  Measured before this line existed:
# 0 of 266 eligible exports emitted, 266 refused as "no declaration found in
# Wine headers".  d3dx9.h is not in the global probe (wine_sig.PROBE_SRC)
# because nothing else needs it, and it is the umbrella that pulls in
# d3dx9math.h, d3dx9core.h, d3dx9tex.h, d3dx9shader.h, d3dx9mesh.h,
# d3dx9anim.h, d3dx9shape.h, d3dx9effect.h and d3dx9xof.h -- that is, the
# entire helper library, which is a header-declared surface with no widl
# involvement at all.  One line takes it to 264 of 266.
#
# WHAT THAT BUYS.  The whole flat D3DX* math library (D3DXMatrix*, D3DXVec*,
# D3DXQuaternion*, D3DXPlane*, D3DXColor*, the SH lighting set), the texture
# and surface loaders (D3DXCreateTextureFromFile*, D3DXLoadSurfaceFromMemory,
# D3DXSaveTextureToFile*, D3DXFilterTexture, ...), the shader compiler
# forwards, the mesh and .x-file entry points and the sprite/font/line/effect
# CREATORS.  Every one of these is pointer-in/pointer-out by construction --
# D3DX math writes through an out-pointer and returns it -- which is why the
# floating-point ceiling this port has (arguments must fit XMM0-3) bites only
# once, below.
#
# This surface does NOT need the d3d9 lane to land first.  A D3DX entry point
# that takes or vends an IDirect3DDevice9 passes the pointer through, and
# whether that pointer is a guest proxy or a native object is decided in
# d3d9's own lane, by whatever winecom instance vends it there.  Nothing here
# has to know.
PROBE-EXTRA   d3dx9.h

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO REFUSALS, BY NAME.  Both are shape, not laziness, and both are
# reported by the generator with these exact texts:
#
#   D3DXSHEvalHemisphereLight
#       "int128 argument (wider than one 64-bit slot) (d3dx9_42.spec:256)"
#       -- the .spec's own argument list says int128.  The descriptor carries
#       one 64-bit slot per argument and the host marshaller reads the guest's
#       integer register file; a 128-bit argument has no slot to arrive in.
#
#   D3DXComputeTangentFrameEx
#       "floating-point argument 11 is past XMM3, which MS-x64 passes on the
#       stack" -- the host reads its floating-point arguments out of the
#       guest's XMM registers (marshal_thunk_args_fp in ntdll), and MS-x64 has
#       only XMM0-3 for arguments.  The twelfth argument is on the guest stack,
#       where nothing on this side looks.
#
# Both are absent from the export table and bind to a distinct named sentinel,
# so a guest that calls one dies at 0xdead00nn with the symbol in the WARN --
# never a silently wrong tangent frame.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INTERFACE HALF, AND WHY IT IS NOT REFUSED HERE.  About sixty of the 264
# vend a COM object -- D3DXCreateTextureFromFile* an IDirect3DTexture9,
# D3DXCreateBuffer an ID3DXBuffer, D3DXCreateEffect an ID3DXEffect,
# D3DXCreateSprite/Font/Line/Mesh their own -- and this module publishes no
# COM roster, so what reaches the guest is a NATIVE vtable.  A guest method
# call through one executes ppc64 bytes as x86-64: exactly the defect
# hangover-ppc64le/docs/system-com-design.md exists for.
#
# It is not reachable today, and the reason is worth writing down rather than
# assuming.  Every one of those entry points needs a live IDirect3DDevice9 as
# its first argument, and a guest has no way to obtain one: dlls/d3d9 has no
# .thunks file at all, so d3d9.dll.Direct3DCreate9 binds a missing-import
# sentinel and the guest dies there, naming the symbol, before it ever holds a
# device.  The hazard therefore opens on exactly the day d3d9's guest lane
# lands -- which is the graphics lane's, not this one's -- and closes the same
# way d3d11's did: one COM-JSON roster naming the D3D9 and D3DX9 interfaces,
# one winecom instance, and this file gaining a COM-JSON line.  Serving the
# flat half now costs that lane nothing and unblocks every caller that only
# wants the math.
#
# ID3DXEffect is the one to expect trouble from when that day comes, and it is
# named here so the next pass starts from a list rather than a crash: 87 slots
# over a surface of D3DXHANDLEs, by-value D3DXVECTOR4/D3DXCOLOR and
# D3DXMATRIX pointers, plus an ID3DXEffectStateManager the APPLICATION
# implements -- a guest-implemented object handed to native code, which is the
# reverse-proxy direction winecom does not have yet (design §6, step 5).  That
# one is a refusal by construction, not by effort.
